WEBVTT

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:06.280
Three tricky moments in the reform conversation.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:10.180
Moment one: Change turns into a complaint session.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.400
Everything is wrong, energy drops, people start blaming others.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:18.800
Bring it back gently: “What part of this is within our control?”

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.120
And move to Try sooner.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:26.940
Complaints are often frustrated wishes; Try turns them into something the team can do.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:31.160
Moment two: the same Change items come back every time.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.760
That’s a strong signal.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:38.960
Either the experiments were too big, nobody followed up, or the real problem sits outside the team.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.080
Name it: “This is the third time we’ve written this.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.820
What’s really stopping us?”

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.620
The honest answer to that question is usually more useful than another experiment.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.080
Moment three: big changes nobody chose.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.620
A reorganisation, a new leader, people leaving.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:02.140
The team may feel frustrated or sad, and Keep / Change / Try can feel too cheerful.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.120
Make room for that first.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:09.760
Ask: “What are we losing, and what do we want to take with us?” Then do the exercise.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.200
One more tip: celebrate experiments that failed.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:18.400
If the team tried something and it didn’t work, that’s learning, not failure. Say so.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.380
Teams that are punished for failed experiments stop experimenting.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:27.420
And if you only remember one thing: the value of this conversation is in the follow-up.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.591
An experiment nobody looks at again is just a wish.

